          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a disorder that affects the whole joint, including cartilage, bone, ligaments, and surrounding muscles. It is characterized by the progressive loss of cartilage and the remodeling of the underlying bone. Commonly affected areas include the knees, hips, hands, and spine. It's important to note that the term \"wear and tear\" is misleading; osteoarthritis involves active inflammation and repair processes, and exercise is often recommended as a first-line treatment, contrary to the idea that resting the joint is beneficial (source: General clinical reference).\n\nAs for the difference between osteoarthritis and arthritis, osteoarthritis is one type of arthritis. \"Arthritis\" is a broader term referring to inflammation of the joints, which can include various conditions, such as rheumatoid arthritis, gout, and osteoarthritis itself.\n\nI did not cover specific details about the management strategies for osteoarthritis or the ICD-10 classification code in this response, but I can provide that information if needed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
